[36m(PPOTrainer pid=454521)[0m INFO 03-02 20:06:37 ppo_trainer.py:457] [trace] step=25 reward=1.000 response_len=1472 total_len=3021 preview='systemYou are ChatGPT, a large language model trained by OpenAI.\\nKnowledge cutoff: 2024-06\\nCurrent date: 2026-03-02\\n\\nReasoning: medium\\n\\n# Valid channels: analysis, commentary, final. Channel must be included for every message.\\nCalls to these tools must go to the commentary channel: \'functions\'.developer# Tools\\n\\n## functions\\n\\nnamespace functions {\\n\\n// Return the average molecular weight (Daltons).\\ntype get_molecular_weight = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string,\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return the monoisotopic exact molecular weight (Daltons).\\ntype get_exact_molecular_weight = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string,\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return the number of non-hydrogen atoms (heavy atoms).\\ntype get_heavy_atom_count = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string,\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return Wildman–Crippen cLogP (octanol/water).\\ntype get_mol_logp = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string,\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return topological polar surface area TPSA (Å^2).\\ntype get_tpsa = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string,\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return Lipinski H-bond donors count.\\ntype get_hbd = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string,\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return Lipinski H-bond acceptors count.\\ntype get_hba = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string,\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return rotatable bonds count.\\ntype get_num_rotatable_bonds = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string,\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return FractionCSP3 = (# sp3 carbon atoms)/(# total carbon atoms), Often used as a simple “3D character / saturation” proxy from 2D structure.\\ntype get_fraction_csp3 = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string,\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return Wildman–Crippen molar refractivity (MR).\\ntype get_mol_mr = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string,\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return total ring count.\\ntype get_ring_count = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string,\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return aromatic ring count.\\ntype get_num_aromatic_rings = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string,\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return net formal charge (sum of atom formal charges as encoded in the SMILES).\\ntype get_formal_charge = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string,\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return QED (Quantitative Estimate of Drug-likeness) as implemented in RDKit.\\ntype get_qed = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string,\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return heteroatom count (non C/H).\\ntype get_num_heteroatoms = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string,\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Accurately parse functional groups with attachment points, corresponding fragment SMILES, and atom ids in the SMILES string for better structure description.\\ntype describe_high_level_fg_fragments = (_: {\\n// The SMILES string to parse.\\nsmiles: string,\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Desalt a SMILES by removing salts/counterions and keeping ONLY the largest organic fragment. Use this tool when the SMILES contains multiple disconnected components separated by \'.\' and one or more components are likely inorganic ions/solvents (e.g., [Na+], Cl-, Br-, I-, [K+], [Li+], [Ca2+], [Mg2+], [NH4+], sulfate/phosphate, etc.), or when you want the parent (active) molecule for downstream property prediction.\\nIn aqueous/physiological conditions, salts typically dissociate; counterions are usually not part of the parent drug structure for many structure-based predictions.\\nTypical examples needing desalting: \'CC(=O)O.[Na+]\', \'[Na+].O=C(O)c1ccccc1\', \'CCO.Cl\'.\\nDo NOT use if the SMILES is already a single component (no \'.\') or if multiple organic fragments represent real mixture/co-crystal/combination therapy that should be kept. Output is the canonical SMILES of the retained parent fragment; smaller fragments are discarded.\\ntype remove_salts = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string,\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Screen a molecule against RDKit\'s built-in structural alert catalogs (PAINS, Brenk, NIH, ZINC, ChEMBL, etc.).\\ntype score_structural_alerts = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string,\\n// Alert library to screen against (default: all).\\nalert_library?: "all" | "pains" | "pains_a" | "pains_b" | "pains_c" | "brenk" | "nih" | "zinc" | "chembl" | "chembl_bms" | "chembl_lint" | "chembl_mlsmr",\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return fraction of aromatic atoms.\\ntype get_fraction_aromatic_atoms = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string,\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return aromatic atom count.\\ntype get_num_aromatic_atoms = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string,\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return max absolute Gasteiger partial charge.\\ntype get_max_abs_partial_charge = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string,\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return min absolute Gasteiger partial charge.\\ntype get_min_abs_partial_charge = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string,\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return max EState index among atoms.\\ntype get_max_estate_index = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string,\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return min EState index among atoms.\\ntype get_min_estate_index = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string,\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return Bertz complexity index.\\ntype get_bertz_ct = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string,\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return Balaban J topological index.\\ntype get_balaban_j = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string,\\n}) => any;\\n\\n} // namespace functionsuserInstructions: Answer the following question about drug properties.\\nContext: Mutagenicity means the ability of a drug to induce genetic alterations. Drugs that can cause damage to the DNA can result in cell death or other severe adverse effects. Nowadays, the most widely used assay for testing the mutagenicity of compounds is the Ames experiment which was invented by a professor named Ames. The Ames test is a short-term bacterial reverse mutation assay detecting a large number of compounds which can induce genetic damage and frameshift mutations.\\nQuestion: Given a drug SMILES string, predict whether it\\n(A) is not mutagenic (B) is mutagenic\\nDrug SMILES: O=C(CO)N(O)c1ccc(Cl)cc1Please think step by step and use tools when helpful. Then put your final choice ((A) or (B)) after "Answer:"assistantanalysisWe need to predict mutagenicity based on SMILES. Likely need to use some rules? We could approximate by structural alerts: presence of certain functional groups may indicate mutagenic potential (e.g., halogenated aromatics, nitro groups, etc.). The compound: O=C(CO)N(O)c1ccc(Cl)cc1. That\'s carboxamide with hydroxy group? Actually it\'s 4-chlorophenoxyacetamide? Let\'s parse: O=C(CO)N(O)c1ccc(Cl)cc1. That\'s 4-chlorophenoxyacetic acid? Wait: O=C(CO)N(O)c1ccc(Cl)cc1. This is an amide where the nitrogen has an OH: N-hydroxy? So it\'s a hydroxamic acid attached to a 4-chloro phenyl. Hydroxamic acids can be reactive; they can chelate metal. Mutagenicity? Not sure.\\n\\nWe might want to use RDKit to compute some descriptors? But we don\'t have RDKit, but we have functions that can compute properties like get_formal_charge, etc. But we don\'t have a function for mutagenicity. So we need to guess. The question likely expects using structural alerts: e.g., presence of halogenated aromatic rings and hydroxamic acid may be mutagenic? Hydroxamic acids are known to be reactive and can inhibit HDACs but not necessarily mutagenic. However, the presence of a chloro substituent on phenyl ring might increase mutagenicity risk. But overall, many small molecules with halogens can be mutagenic.\\n\\nMaybe we can use the "score_structural_alerts" function to see if it triggers any alerts (PAINS, etc.). Let\'s try that.assistantcommentary to=functions.score_structural_alerts json{"smiles":"O=C(CO)N(O)c1ccc(Cl)cc1"}functions.score_structural_alerts to=assistantcommentary{"result": "{\\n  \\"library\\": \\"all\\",\\n  \\"count\\": 7,\\n  \\"alerts\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\"description\\": \\"hydroxamic_acid\\",\\n      \\"filter_set\\": \\"Brenk\\",\\n      \\"scope\\": \\"unwanted functionality due to potential tox reasons or unfavourable pharmacokinetic properties\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\"description\\": \\"Oxygen-nitrogen_single_bond\\",\\n      \\"filter_set\\": \\"Brenk\\",\\n      \\"scope\\": \\"unwanted functionality due to potential tox reasons or unfavourable pharmacokinetic properties\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\"description\\": \\"hydroxamic acid\\",\\n      \\"filter_set\\": \\"ChEMBL23_Dundee\\",\\n      \\"scope\\": \\"ChEMBL 23 structural alerts rule-set: CHEMBL_Dundee\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\"description\\": \\"Oxygen-nitrogen single bond\\",\\n      \\"filter_set\\": \\"ChEMBL23_Dundee\\",\\n      \\"scope\\": \\"ChEMBL 23 structural alerts rule-set: CHEMBL_Dundee\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\"description\\": \\"Nitrosone_not_nitro\\",\\n      \\"filter_set\\": \\"ChEMBL23_SureChEMBL\\",\\n      \\"scope\\": \\"ChEMBL 23 structural alerts rule-set: CHEMBL_SureChEMBL\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\"description\\": \\"Filter89_hydroxylamine\\",\\n      \\"filter_set\\": \\"ChEMBL23_Inpharmatica\\",\\n      \\"scope\\": \\"ChEMBL 23 structural alerts rule-set: CHEMBL_Inpharmatica\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\"description\\": \\"acyclic NO not nitro\\",\\n      \\"filter_set\\": \\"ChEMBL23_LINT\\",\\n      \\"scope\\": \\"ChEMBL 23 structural alerts rule-set: CHEMBL_LINT\\"\\n    }\\n  ]\\n}", "function_name": "score_structural_alerts", "arguments": {"smiles": "O=C(CO)N(O)c1ccc(Cl)cc1"}}assistantanalysisThe alerts show hydroxamic acid, oxygen-nitrogen single bond, etc. Not necessarily mutagenicity but possibly toxic. However, the question is simple: is it mutagenic? We can guess that hydroxamic acids are known to be potential mutagens? But I\'m not sure.\\n\\nLet\'s consider the Ames test: compounds that can form reactive intermediates that damage DNA. Hydroxamic acids can form radicals? But not typical. But halogenated aromatic compounds can be mutagenic.\\n\\nHowever, the compound is relatively small and not strongly reactive. The presence of a chloro substituent might increase mutagenicity but not high.\\n\\nMaybe we can use the "get_qed" or other property? Not relevant.\\n\\nWe might use the "get_formal_charge" to see if it\'s neutral. Let\'s compute.assistantcommentary to=functions.get_formal_charge json{"smiles":"O=C(CO)N(O)c1ccc(Cl)cc1"}functions.get_formal_charge to=assistantcommentary{"result": "Net formal charge: 0", "function_name": "get_formal_charge", "arguments": {"smiles": "O=C(CO)N(O)c1ccc(Cl)cc1"}}assistantanalysisNow, maybe we can use the "get_molecular_weight".assistantcommentary to=functions.get_molecular_weight json{"smiles":"O=C(CO)N(O)c1ccc(Cl)cc1"}functions.get_molecular_weight to=assistantcommentary{"result": "Average molecular weight (Daltons): 201.6090", "function_name": "get_molecular_weight", "arguments": {"smiles": "O=C(CO)N(O)c1ccc(Cl)cc1"}}assistantanalysisBut this doesn\'t help.\\n\\nWe might guess that the compound is not strongly mutagenic. Let\'s think: The compound is similar to 4-chlorophenoxyacetic acid (C4Cl phenoxyacetic acid). That is a herbicide? Actually 4-chlorophenoxyacetic acid is a plant hormone. Not known to be mutagenic. Hydroxamic acid maybe not mutagenic. So answer likely (A) not mutagenic.\\n\\nBut we should check literature: Hydroxamic acids can be metabolized to reactive species? Some are mutagenic? But not sure.\\n\\nWe can also check for "Ames test" predictions: Many small molecules with halogens can be mutagenic. But the presence of hydroxamic acid may reduce.\\n\\nGiven the limited info, I\'d lean to not mutagenic. So answer (A).assistantfinalAnswer: (A)'
